  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nLate evening water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal strong ridging is in place atop the Ark-La-Tex \nregion, with a potent cut-off low over the northwestern CONUS. At \nthe surface, a lee cyclone remains in place across eastern CO and \nadjacent areas, which has supported south-southwesterly winds across \nthe central plains the past couple days. Friday will be no different \nas winds become sustained in the 15-25 mph range gusting to 35 mph \nduring the late morning through early evening hours. These \ndownsloping winds along with strong solar insolation will contribute \nto afternoon highs in the mid/upper 90s once again. HREF members \nimply the majority of southwest KS will remain dry Friday afternoon \nand evening under the influence of the strong upper ridge, however a \nfew stray showers/thunderstorms are possible favoring our northwest \nzones. Overnight, elevated southerly winds will limit Saturday \nmorning lows to the mid 60s to mid 70s.\n\nDaytime Saturday, short range ensembles agree the aforementioned \npotent upper trough will open up and advance eastward to the \nnorthern Rockies, and this will coincide with a slight weakening of \nthe ridge over the southern plains. Despite the subtle height falls \nand a cold front impinging on our area, pre-frontal downslope flow \nand plenty of sun will support one final day of afternoon highs in \nthe 90s. This boundary may serve as the focus for thunderstorm \ndevelopment Saturday afternoon/evening favoring roughly the \nsoutheast half of our area, however HREF members exhibit plenty of \ndisagreement on several factors.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe upper trough will continue to track along the northern CONUS, \nfurther dampening the ridge in the process. In the wake of a strong \ncold fropa late Saturday night/early Sunday morning, and under lower \n500-mb heights and westerly flow, southwest KS will enjoy its first \nprolonged stretch of fall-like temperatures this season, as \nafternoon highs stay in the mid 70s to mid 80s range through at \nleast Thursday. Several opportunities for precipitation will also \nexist, most notably with the frontal passage early Sunday morning \nwhen NBM has widespread pops in the chance (25-54%), likely \n(55-74%), and definite (75-100%) categories.",
